120 answers from one run of Clarity v1.0 — the tokens each agent loop reported,
not counting the model calls its tools make inside them

  mean       1,465 tokens
  p50         870 tokens
  p90       2,971 tokens
  p99       6,746 tokens
  largest    8,610 tokens   (9.9x the median)

  the most expensive tenth of answers used 34% of all tokens

  population        n   median      p90  share of tokens
  document         74      853    1,221              49%
  unanswerable     20    2,800    5,219              35%
  warehouse        20      838      878              10%
  composite         6    1,493    2,900               6%

The mean is 1.7x the median: a few long runs pull it up, so a budget
set from the mean is too generous for most requests and too tight for the ones
that need it. Budget per request from a high percentile, and look at who lives there.

Here it is the unanswerable questions: 17% of the requests and 35% of the tokens.
An agent searches hardest for what is not there, so the cheapest refusal is the
one decided early — which is a cost argument for §13.11's abstention, not only a
quality one.
